1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,503
Sweetheart, in nineteen fifty-four,

2
00:00:02,503 --> 00:00:05,078
when the plant cut my father's hours

3
00:00:05,078 --> 00:00:06,580
to three days a week,

4
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:09,227
we ate pinto beans and cornbread four

5
00:00:09,227 --> 00:00:10,657
nights out of seven.

6
00:00:10,837 --> 00:00:12,912
And I'm going to tell you a

7
00:00:12,912 --> 00:00:13,450
secret.

8
00:00:13,630 --> 00:00:15,708
I didn't know we were poor.

9
00:00:15,888 --> 00:00:18,092
I thought it was the best supper

10
00:00:18,092 --> 00:00:18,988
in the world.

11
00:00:19,168 --> 00:00:21,042
A pot of beans with a ham

12
00:00:21,042 --> 00:00:23,217
hock, a skillet of cornbread,

13
00:00:23,217 --> 00:00:25,542
and a glass of cold buttermilk.

14
00:00:25,892 --> 00:00:27,039
But these days,

15
00:00:27,039 --> 00:00:29,793
people tell me their beans never get

16
00:00:29,793 --> 00:00:30,176
soft.

17
00:00:30,356 --> 00:00:32,895
They cook them for hours and they're

18
00:00:32,895 --> 00:00:34,659
still hard in the middle,

19
00:00:34,659 --> 00:00:36,775
or they split and turn to mush

20
00:00:36,775 --> 00:00:39,667
on the outside while they're hard inside.

21
00:00:39,847 --> 00:00:42,891
So tonight I'm giving you Grandma Pearl's

22
00:00:42,891 --> 00:00:43,783
pinto beans.

23
00:00:43,963 --> 00:00:46,660
Creamy, tender, every single bean,

24
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:49,199
with a rich broth you'll want to

25
00:00:49,199 --> 00:00:49,675
drink.

26
00:00:49,855 --> 00:00:53,209
Every step, and the four reasons beans

27
00:00:53,209 --> 00:00:54,092
stay hard.

28
00:00:54,272 --> 00:00:55,489
And stay with me,

29
00:00:55,489 --> 00:00:58,210
because reason number three is the one

30
00:00:58,210 --> 00:01:01,145
nobody believes until it happens to them.

31
00:01:01,496 --> 00:01:04,049
Grandma Pearl kept a brown crock bean

32
00:01:04,049 --> 00:01:05,982
pot on the back of her stove

33
00:01:05,982 --> 00:01:07,777
for as long as I knew her.

34
00:01:07,957 --> 00:01:09,558
During the Depression,

35
00:01:09,558 --> 00:01:11,961
a pound of dried pinto beans cost

36
00:01:11,961 --> 00:01:13,054
about a nickel,

37
00:01:13,054 --> 00:01:15,165
and it fed her family of nine

38
00:01:15,165 --> 00:01:16,258
with cornbread.

39
00:01:16,438 --> 00:01:19,506
She said, beans and cornbread together are

40
00:01:19,506 --> 00:01:20,675
as good as meat.

41
00:01:20,855 --> 00:01:22,423
And she was right,

42
00:01:22,423 --> 00:01:22,945
honey.

43
00:01:23,125 --> 00:01:25,842
The doctors say the same thing today.

44
00:01:26,192 --> 00:01:29,003
Every Monday, Pearl picked over a pound

45
00:01:29,003 --> 00:01:31,165
of beans at the kitchen table,

46
00:01:31,165 --> 00:01:32,968
put them to soak in a big

47
00:01:32,968 --> 00:01:35,419
bowl, and covered them with a dish

48
00:01:35,419 --> 00:01:35,851
towel.

49
00:01:36,031 --> 00:01:37,316
On Tuesday morning,

50
00:01:37,316 --> 00:01:39,751
they went on the stove with whatever

51
00:01:39,751 --> 00:01:40,292
she had.

52
00:01:40,472 --> 00:01:42,581
A ham bone from Sunday,

53
00:01:42,581 --> 00:01:44,507
a piece of salt pork,

54
00:01:44,507 --> 00:01:46,799
or a few slices of bacon.

55
00:01:46,979 --> 00:01:50,279
By supper, the whole farmhouse smelled like

56
00:01:50,279 --> 00:01:51,507
beans and smoke.

57
00:01:51,858 --> 00:01:53,074
When I was nine,

58
00:01:53,074 --> 00:01:55,050
she let me do the picking.

59
00:01:55,230 --> 00:01:57,687
She poured the beans on a white

60
00:01:57,687 --> 00:01:59,827
plate, a handful at a time,

61
00:01:59,827 --> 00:02:02,363
and I pushed them across with my

62
00:02:02,363 --> 00:02:05,057
finger, looking for little stones,

63
00:02:05,057 --> 00:02:07,990
broken beans, and the shriveled ones.

64
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:10,203
I found a stone the size of

65
00:02:10,203 --> 00:02:11,031
a pea once,

66
00:02:11,031 --> 00:02:13,064
and she gave me a penny for

67
00:02:13,064 --> 00:02:13,290
it.

68
00:02:13,470 --> 00:02:15,718
She said, that's a penny and a

69
00:02:15,718 --> 00:02:16,918
tooth you saved.

70
00:02:17,268 --> 00:02:19,019
Here's what you need,

71
00:02:19,019 --> 00:02:19,520
honey.

72
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:22,777
One pound of dried pinto beans.

73
00:02:22,957 --> 00:02:24,698
One smoked ham hock,

74
00:02:24,698 --> 00:02:26,439
or a meaty ham bone,

75
00:02:26,439 --> 00:02:29,052
or four slices of thick bacon.

76
00:02:29,232 --> 00:02:31,229
One onion, chopped.

77
00:02:31,409 --> 00:02:33,080
Three cloves of garlic.

78
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:34,584
One bay leaf.

79
00:02:34,764 --> 00:02:36,900
About ten cups of water.

80
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:38,810
And salt and pepper.

81
00:02:38,990 --> 00:02:40,058
That's it.

82
00:02:40,238 --> 00:02:42,749
If you want them the Texas way,

83
00:02:42,749 --> 00:02:45,097
add a teaspoon of cumin and a

84
00:02:45,097 --> 00:02:46,717
little chili powder.

85
00:02:47,067 --> 00:02:50,619
Now, the most important ingredient isn't on

86
00:02:50,619 --> 00:02:51,362
the list.

87
00:02:51,542 --> 00:02:53,260
It's fresh beans.

88
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:56,038
Dried beans don't last forever,

89
00:02:56,038 --> 00:02:56,540
honey.

90
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:58,602
After a year or two on the

91
00:02:58,602 --> 00:03:00,845
shelf, they get so dry and hard

92
00:03:00,845 --> 00:03:02,726
that they'll never soften,

93
00:03:02,726 --> 00:03:05,114
no matter how long you cook them.

94
00:03:05,294 --> 00:03:07,393
If that bag has been in your

95
00:03:07,393 --> 00:03:09,791
pantry since the last president,

96
00:03:09,791 --> 00:03:12,040
throw it out and buy new ones.

97
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:14,280
In a minute I'll show you how

98
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:16,625
to tell fresh beans from old ones

99
00:03:16,625 --> 00:03:17,549
at the store.

100
00:03:17,899 --> 00:03:19,152
How do you tell?

101
00:03:19,332 --> 00:03:21,407
Fresh pinto beans are bright,

102
00:03:21,407 --> 00:03:23,052
speckled, pink and tan,

103
00:03:23,052 --> 00:03:25,126
and they have a little shine.

104
00:03:25,306 --> 00:03:27,734
Old beans look faded and dull,

105
00:03:27,734 --> 00:03:29,516
sometimes almost gray,

106
00:03:29,516 --> 00:03:31,297
and some are wrinkled.

107
00:03:31,477 --> 00:03:33,773
Buy them from a busy store that

108
00:03:33,773 --> 00:03:35,329
sells a lot of beans,

109
00:03:35,329 --> 00:03:37,699
where the bags don't sit around.

110
00:03:37,879 --> 00:03:39,596
In the stores near me,

111
00:03:39,596 --> 00:03:43,184
the Mexican groceries always have the freshest

112
00:03:43,184 --> 00:03:44,277
beans in town.

113
00:03:44,627 --> 00:03:46,356
Step one, the soak.

114
00:03:46,536 --> 00:03:48,077
Pick through the beans,

115
00:03:48,077 --> 00:03:50,020
rinse them, and put them in a

116
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:52,297
big bowl with enough cold water to

117
00:03:52,297 --> 00:03:54,106
cover them by three inches.

118
00:03:54,286 --> 00:03:56,062
They'll double in size.

119
00:03:56,242 --> 00:03:57,790
Leave them overnight,

120
00:03:57,790 --> 00:03:59,412
eight to twelve hours.

121
00:03:59,592 --> 00:04:02,472
Soaking makes them cook faster and more

122
00:04:02,472 --> 00:04:04,983
evenly, so you don't get the mushy

123
00:04:04,983 --> 00:04:06,755
outside and hard middle.

124
00:04:07,106 --> 00:04:09,300
If you forgot to soak them,

125
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:11,169
my mother's quick soak.

126
00:04:11,349 --> 00:04:13,065
Put the beans in a pot,

127
00:04:13,065 --> 00:04:14,707
cover them with water,

128
00:04:14,707 --> 00:04:16,796
bring them to a boil for two

129
00:04:16,796 --> 00:04:19,183
minutes, then turn off the heat,

130
00:04:19,183 --> 00:04:20,303
put the lid on,

131
00:04:20,303 --> 00:04:22,541
and let them sit for one hour.

132
00:04:22,721 --> 00:04:25,067
That's almost as good as a whole

133
00:04:25,067 --> 00:04:25,507
night.

134
00:04:25,687 --> 00:04:26,937
Then drain them,

135
00:04:26,937 --> 00:04:30,060
rinse them, and start cooking with fresh

136
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:30,529
water.

137
00:04:30,709 --> 00:04:32,419
If this is helping you,

138
00:04:32,419 --> 00:04:34,946
honey, tap the like button for me.

139
00:04:35,126 --> 00:04:37,669
It helps Grandma more than you know.

140
00:04:38,019 --> 00:04:40,437
And here's a little secret for hard

141
00:04:40,437 --> 00:04:40,851
water.

142
00:04:41,031 --> 00:04:43,649
If your water leaves white spots on

143
00:04:43,649 --> 00:04:45,294
the faucet, it's hard,

144
00:04:45,294 --> 00:04:47,986
and hard water keeps beans hard too.

145
00:04:48,166 --> 00:04:50,465
The minerals toughen the skins.

146
00:04:50,645 --> 00:04:53,212
Add a small pinch of baking soda,

147
00:04:53,212 --> 00:04:55,547
about an eighth of a teaspoon,

148
00:04:55,547 --> 00:04:57,181
to the soaking water.

149
00:04:57,361 --> 00:04:58,499
Just a pinch.

150
00:04:58,679 --> 00:05:01,075
More than that and the beans taste

151
00:05:01,075 --> 00:05:02,696
soapy and turn to mush.

152
00:05:03,046 --> 00:05:04,009
Step two.

153
00:05:04,189 --> 00:05:06,952
Drain the soaked beans and rinse them.

154
00:05:07,132 --> 00:05:08,734
In your big heavy pot,

155
00:05:08,734 --> 00:05:10,772
cook the onion in a spoon of

156
00:05:10,772 --> 00:05:13,392
bacon grease or oil until it's soft,

157
00:05:13,392 --> 00:05:15,503
then the garlic for a minute.

158
00:05:15,683 --> 00:05:16,912
Add the beans,

159
00:05:16,912 --> 00:05:18,053
the ham hock,

160
00:05:18,053 --> 00:05:19,193
the bay leaf,

161
00:05:19,193 --> 00:05:21,651
and ten cups of fresh water.

162
00:05:21,831 --> 00:05:23,481
Bring it up to a boil,

163
00:05:23,481 --> 00:05:25,732
then turn it right down to the

164
00:05:25,732 --> 00:05:28,433
lowest simmer, just a bubble now and

165
00:05:28,433 --> 00:05:28,808
then.

166
00:05:29,159 --> 00:05:31,307
This is mistake number two,

167
00:05:31,307 --> 00:05:34,012
the one that ruins the most beans.

168
00:05:34,192 --> 00:05:35,573
Boiling them hard.

169
00:05:35,753 --> 00:05:38,916
A rolling boil bangs the beans around,

170
00:05:38,916 --> 00:05:40,330
splits the skins,

171
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:43,410
and turns the outsides to mush before

172
00:05:43,410 --> 00:05:45,157
the insides are done.

173
00:05:45,337 --> 00:05:47,381
Beans want a lazy simmer.

174
00:05:47,561 --> 00:05:48,596
Put the lid on,

175
00:05:48,596 --> 00:05:49,701
tilted a little,

176
00:05:49,701 --> 00:05:51,841
and leave them alone for two to

177
00:05:51,841 --> 00:05:52,669
three hours.

178
00:05:52,849 --> 00:05:54,651
Stir every half hour,

179
00:05:54,651 --> 00:05:55,252
gently.

180
00:05:55,603 --> 00:05:58,234
And keep them covered with liquid the

181
00:05:58,234 --> 00:05:59,016
whole time.

182
00:05:59,196 --> 00:06:01,546
If the water drops below the top

183
00:06:01,546 --> 00:06:02,501
of the beans,

184
00:06:02,501 --> 00:06:04,704
add hot water from the kettle,

185
00:06:04,704 --> 00:06:05,512
never cold.

186
00:06:05,692 --> 00:06:07,529
Cold water shocks them,

187
00:06:07,529 --> 00:06:10,324
and Pearl swore it made them tough.

188
00:06:10,504 --> 00:06:12,185
I've never tested it,

189
00:06:12,185 --> 00:06:15,548
honey, because I've never dared go against

190
00:06:15,548 --> 00:06:16,669
Grandma Pearl.

191
00:06:16,849 --> 00:06:17,944
Before I go on,

192
00:06:17,944 --> 00:06:20,573
the salt, because an old wives' tale

193
00:06:20,573 --> 00:06:22,909
has fooled people about it for a

194
00:06:22,909 --> 00:06:23,931
hundred years.

195
00:06:24,281 --> 00:06:26,080
Step three, the salt.

196
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:27,665
When do you add it?

197
00:06:27,845 --> 00:06:30,354
Pearl added salt at the start,

198
00:06:30,354 --> 00:06:31,358
and so do I,

199
00:06:31,358 --> 00:06:33,868
a teaspoon and a half with the

200
00:06:33,868 --> 00:06:34,370
water.

201
00:06:34,550 --> 00:06:37,158
People will tell you salt keeps beans

202
00:06:37,158 --> 00:06:37,510
hard.

203
00:06:37,690 --> 00:06:39,629
That's an old wives' tale.

204
00:06:39,809 --> 00:06:42,444
Salt in the water actually makes the

205
00:06:42,444 --> 00:06:45,151
skins softer and the beans season all

206
00:06:45,151 --> 00:06:46,322
the way through.

207
00:06:46,502 --> 00:06:49,489
Beans salted only at the end taste

208
00:06:49,489 --> 00:06:50,543
flat inside.

209
00:06:50,893 --> 00:06:53,771
But what really does keep beans hard

210
00:06:53,771 --> 00:06:54,410
is acid.

211
00:06:54,590 --> 00:06:57,601
Tomatoes, vinegar, ketchup,

212
00:06:57,601 --> 00:06:59,385
molasses, lemon.

213
00:06:59,565 --> 00:07:01,591
If you add any of those at

214
00:07:01,591 --> 00:07:04,240
the start, your beans can cook all

215
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:06,032
day and never get soft.

216
00:07:06,212 --> 00:07:08,499
That's mistake number three.

217
00:07:08,679 --> 00:07:11,772
Wait until the beans are completely tender,

218
00:07:11,772 --> 00:07:14,074
and then stir in the tomatoes or

219
00:07:14,074 --> 00:07:14,937
the vinegar.

220
00:07:15,117 --> 00:07:17,799
That's why baked beans take so long.

221
00:07:17,979 --> 00:07:20,728
They're full of molasses and tomato from

222
00:07:20,728 --> 00:07:21,415
the start.

223
00:07:21,766 --> 00:07:23,554
How do you know they're done?

224
00:07:23,734 --> 00:07:26,352
Scoop out five beans on a spoon,

225
00:07:26,352 --> 00:07:27,415
blow on them,

226
00:07:27,415 --> 00:07:28,970
and taste all five.

227
00:07:29,150 --> 00:07:30,148
Not one.

228
00:07:30,328 --> 00:07:31,083
Five.

229
00:07:31,263 --> 00:07:33,323
If every one is creamy all the

230
00:07:33,323 --> 00:07:35,590
way through, with no chalk in the

231
00:07:35,590 --> 00:07:37,033
middle, they're done.

232
00:07:37,213 --> 00:07:38,790
If even one is firm,

233
00:07:38,790 --> 00:07:40,998
give them another half hour.

234
00:07:41,178 --> 00:07:43,258
Then take out the ham hock,

235
00:07:43,258 --> 00:07:44,645
pull off the meat,

236
00:07:44,645 --> 00:07:46,726
chop it, and put it back in

237
00:07:46,726 --> 00:07:47,343
the pot.

238
00:07:47,693 --> 00:07:49,399
Pearl's last trick.

239
00:07:49,579 --> 00:07:51,534
Take one cup of beans out,

240
00:07:51,534 --> 00:07:53,188
mash them with a fork,

241
00:07:53,188 --> 00:07:55,593
and stir them back into the pot.

242
00:07:55,773 --> 00:07:58,729
The broth turns thick and creamy and

243
00:07:58,729 --> 00:07:59,221
silky.

244
00:07:59,401 --> 00:08:01,514
Then simmer with the lid off for

245
00:08:01,514 --> 00:08:02,571
fifteen minutes.

246
00:08:02,751 --> 00:08:04,876
Taste for salt and pepper.

247
00:08:05,056 --> 00:08:06,435
That broth, honey,

248
00:08:06,435 --> 00:08:08,658
is what we called pot likker,

249
00:08:08,658 --> 00:08:11,111
and we sopped up every drop with

250
00:08:11,111 --> 00:08:12,184
the cornbread.

251
00:08:12,534 --> 00:08:15,326
So the four reasons beans stay hard,

252
00:08:15,326 --> 00:08:16,179
sweetheart.

253
00:08:16,359 --> 00:08:18,472
One, old beans.

254
00:08:18,652 --> 00:08:19,929
Buy fresh.

255
00:08:20,109 --> 00:08:23,066
Two, boiling them hard instead of a

256
00:08:23,066 --> 00:08:24,080
lazy simmer.

257
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:26,467
Three, acid too early,

258
00:08:26,467 --> 00:08:29,879
the tomatoes, vinegar or molasses.

259
00:08:30,059 --> 00:08:31,801
Wait until they're soft.

260
00:08:31,981 --> 00:08:34,221
And four, hard water.

261
00:08:34,401 --> 00:08:36,795
A tiny pinch of baking soda in

262
00:08:36,795 --> 00:08:37,513
the soak.

263
00:08:37,693 --> 00:08:40,140
Fix those four things and your beans

264
00:08:40,140 --> 00:08:42,383
will be creamy every single time.

265
00:08:42,563 --> 00:08:43,729
But I'm not done,

266
00:08:43,729 --> 00:08:46,131
because coming up is the other half

267
00:08:46,131 --> 00:08:47,091
of the supper.

268
00:08:47,441 --> 00:08:50,540
And you can't have beans without cornbread.

269
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:53,674
Put two tablespoons of bacon grease in

270
00:08:53,674 --> 00:08:56,006
a cast iron skillet and put it

271
00:08:56,006 --> 00:08:59,193
in a four hundred twenty-five degree oven

272
00:08:59,193 --> 00:09:00,670
to get smoking hot.

273
00:09:00,850 --> 00:09:04,478
Meanwhile, stir together one cup of cornmeal,

274
00:09:04,478 --> 00:09:05,849
one cup of flour,

275
00:09:05,849 --> 00:09:08,268
a tablespoon of baking powder,

276
00:09:08,268 --> 00:09:10,686
a teaspoon of salt and a spoon

277
00:09:10,686 --> 00:09:13,266
of sugar if you're a Northerner,

278
00:09:13,266 --> 00:09:13,911
like me.

279
00:09:14,262 --> 00:09:16,141
Then one egg and one and a

280
00:09:16,141 --> 00:09:18,093
quarter cups of buttermilk,

281
00:09:18,093 --> 00:09:20,624
and a quarter cup of melted butter.

282
00:09:20,804 --> 00:09:23,137
Stir just until it comes together.

283
00:09:23,317 --> 00:09:25,605
Pour the batter into the hot skillet.

284
00:09:25,785 --> 00:09:27,665
It should sizzle loud.

285
00:09:27,845 --> 00:09:30,616
That sizzle is what makes the crunchy

286
00:09:30,616 --> 00:09:31,514
brown crust.

287
00:09:31,694 --> 00:09:33,343
Bake for twenty minutes,

288
00:09:33,343 --> 00:09:35,061
until it's golden on top.

289
00:09:35,241 --> 00:09:37,079
Cut it in wedges right in the

290
00:09:37,079 --> 00:09:37,586
skillet.

291
00:09:37,937 --> 00:09:40,309
Serve the beans in deep bowls with

292
00:09:40,309 --> 00:09:42,122
the cornbread on the side,

293
00:09:42,122 --> 00:09:43,867
or crumbled right on top,

294
00:09:43,867 --> 00:09:45,611
the way my father ate it.

295
00:09:45,791 --> 00:09:47,589
Chopped raw onion on top,

296
00:09:47,589 --> 00:09:49,820
and a spoon of chow chow relish

297
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:50,899
if you have it.

298
00:09:51,079 --> 00:09:53,691
The leftovers keep five days in the

299
00:09:53,691 --> 00:09:56,304
icebox and freeze for three months.

300
00:09:56,484 --> 00:09:58,157
And on the third day,

301
00:09:58,157 --> 00:10:00,388
mash them up and fry them in

302
00:10:00,388 --> 00:10:02,858
bacon grease for refried beans.

303
00:10:03,208 --> 00:10:04,778
So one more time,

304
00:10:04,778 --> 00:10:05,332
honey.

305
00:10:05,512 --> 00:10:09,065
Fresh beans, picked over and soaked overnight.

306
00:10:09,245 --> 00:10:11,358
A ham hock and an onion.

307
00:10:11,538 --> 00:10:13,047
Salt at the start.

308
00:10:13,227 --> 00:10:14,768
The lowest simmer,

309
00:10:14,768 --> 00:10:16,395
two to three hours,

310
00:10:16,395 --> 00:10:18,707
lid tilted, hot water only.

311
00:10:18,887 --> 00:10:22,068
No tomatoes or vinegar until they're soft.

312
00:10:22,248 --> 00:10:23,978
Taste five beans.

313
00:10:24,158 --> 00:10:26,782
Mash a cup for a creamy broth.

314
00:10:26,962 --> 00:10:29,295
And a skillet of cornbread with a

315
00:10:29,295 --> 00:10:30,143
loud sizzle.

316
00:10:30,323 --> 00:10:32,775
That's the supper I grew up on,

317
00:10:32,775 --> 00:10:35,305
and I'd still choose it over any

318
00:10:35,305 --> 00:10:35,780
steak.

319
00:10:36,130 --> 00:10:38,072
Tell me in the comments,

320
00:10:38,072 --> 00:10:38,962
sweetheart.

321
00:10:39,142 --> 00:10:40,954
Sugar in your cornbread,

322
00:10:40,954 --> 00:10:41,859
or no sugar?

323
00:10:42,039 --> 00:10:44,538
I know that one starts arguments at

324
00:10:44,538 --> 00:10:46,822
every family reunion in America.

325
00:10:47,002 --> 00:10:48,721
I read every comment.

326
00:10:48,901 --> 00:10:51,707
Every day I'm bringing you another recipe

327
00:10:51,707 --> 00:10:52,802
from my kitchen.

328
00:10:52,982 --> 00:10:55,574
And if you want another one tonight,

329
00:10:55,574 --> 00:10:58,598
the whole playlist of Grandma's recipes is

330
00:10:58,598 --> 00:11:00,542
right there on your screen,

331
00:11:00,542 --> 00:11:02,557
and I'll see you in the next

332
00:11:02,557 --> 00:11:02,989
video.

333
00:11:03,169 --> 00:11:04,772
Good night, honey.
